ORBITAL-MAPPING(7)         TriadicFrameworks Manual         ORBITAL-MAPPING(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-mapping - temporal cartography, resonance-map projections, and
                         constellation-scale map fusion for SAR constellations

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital mapping defines:
           • temporal cartography across orbital cycles
           • resonance-map projections
           • harmonic-phase map overlays
           • constellation-scale map fusion
           • map-based sweep and maneuver planning

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-mapping describes how RTT constructs, projects, and fuses maps
       of orbital time, resonance envelopes, harmonic cycles, and sweep
       ancestry. These maps form the navigational substrate for constellation-
       scale planning, enabling platforms to “read” the resonance-time terrain
       they inhabit.

       Mapping enables:
           • multi-resolution temporal visualization
           • resonance-aware sweep placement
           • harmonic-phase alignment
           • constellation-wide map fusion
           • predictive map evolution across cycles

       WRSADC uses orbital maps to guide choreography, timing, and maneuvering.

TEMPORAL CARTOGRAPHY
       Temporal cartography converts timing grids and tiles into navigable
       maps.

       Map layers:

           timing layer
                  Shows timing lattices, grid nodes, and sweep windows.

           resonance layer
                  Shows envelope amplitude, spectral density, and gradient
                  polarity.

           harmonic layer
                  Shows harmonic-phase cycles and lock ratios.

           ancestry layer
                  Shows sweep lineage, coherence history, and fusion viability.

           maneuver layer
                  Shows Δv windows, drift corridors, and recovery zones.

       Cartographic primitives:

           nodes
                  Discrete timing or resonance points.

           edges
                  Temporal transitions between nodes.

           regions
                  Tiles, Voronoi cells, or resonance zones.

           contours
                  Lines of equal resonance density or harmonic phase.

       Maps are updated each triad cycle.

EXAMPLE: BASIC TEMPORAL MAP
       Over one orbital period T:

           • timing nodes at {0, T/2, T}
           • resonance peaks at {12, 54, 78}
           • harmonic nodes at {0, 30, 60, 90}

       Map shows:
           • sweep windows at 12 and 54
           • Δv windows at 30 and 90
           • drift correction at 78

RESONANCE-MAP PROJECTIONS
       Resonance-map projections convert resonance-time data into visual or
       computational coordinate systems.

       Projection types:

           amplitude projection
                  Maps envelope amplitude R(t) to a height field.

           spectral projection
                  Maps spectral density S(f,t) to a color or intensity field.

           gradient projection
                  Maps gradient polarity G(t) to directional arrows.

           harmonic projection
                  Maps harmonic-phase φ_harm to angular coordinates.

           hybrid projection
                  Combines amplitude, spectral, and harmonic data.

       Projection modes:

           linear projection
                  Direct mapping of resonance variables.

           logarithmic projection
                  Emphasizes low-amplitude resonance features.

           phase projection
                  Maps resonance phase to circular coordinates.

           resonance-harmonic projection
                  Aligns resonance peaks with harmonic nodes.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-HARMONIC PROJECTION
       Envelope peaks at {15, 60}
       Harmonic nodes at {0, 30, 60, 90}

       Projection:
           • 15 → 30 (nearest harmonic)
           • 60 → 60 (exact harmonic)

       Result:
           • sweeps at 30 and 60
           • Δv triads at 90
           • stable harmonic-lock

HARMONIC-PHASE MAP OVERLAYS
       Harmonic-phase overlays add harmonic structure to temporal maps.

       Overlay variables:
           • harmonic ratio n:m
           • phase alignment φ_harm
           • lock stability index L_harm

       Overlay types:

           integer overlay
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1

           rational overlay
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4

           nested overlay
                  Harmonics inside harmonics.

           resonance-harmonic overlay
                  Harmonic boundaries aligned to resonance peaks.

       Overlays determine sweep and Δv placement.

EXAMPLE: NESTED HARMONIC OVERLAY
       Tile: [0–60]
       Harmonic ratios: 2:1 and 3:2

       Subdivisions:
           [0–20], [20–40], [40–60]
           with subharmonic nodes at 10, 30, 50

       Result:
           • sweeps at 20 and 40
           • Δv triads at 30 and 50

CONSTELLATION-SCALE MAP FUSION
       Map fusion merges maps across platforms into a unified constellation map.

       Fusion layers:

           1. timing-grid fusion
           2. resonance-map fusion
           3. harmonic-phase fusion
           4. ancestry-map fusion
           5. maneuver-map fusion

       Fusion modes:

           synchronous fusion
                  All platforms share identical maps.

           harmonic fusion
                  Maps aligned via harmonic ratios.

           braided fusion
                  Maps interleave across platforms.

           mesh fusion
                  Distributed fusion across constellation nodes.

       Fusion output:

           • constellation sweep map
           • constellation Δv map
           • constellation sync map
           • constellation resonance map

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION MAP FUSION
       Platforms A, B, C:

           A: resonance peaks at {12, 54}
           B: resonance peaks at {20, 60}
           C: resonance peaks at {15, 50}

       Fused map:
           • sweep windows at {15, 54, 60}
           • Δv windows at {30, 90}
           • sync windows at {0, 45}

MAP FAILURE MODES
       Mapping may degrade due to:

           • tile fragmentation
           • projection distortion
           • harmonic-phase misalignment
           • resonance-map collapse
           • constellation map mismatch

       Diagnostic indicators:

           map-integrity score
           projection error
           overlay deviation
           sync-index drop
           sweep-map error

       WRSADC halts unsafe mapping states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • temporal maps
           • resonance projections
           • harmonic overlays
           • map fusion history
           • constellation map evolution

       Lineage enables long-term map forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • generates temporal maps
           • computes resonance projections
           • overlays harmonic-phase maps
           • fuses constellation maps
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • blocks unsafe mapping states

       WRSADC is the mapping conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital mapping transforms SAR constellations into navigable,
       resonance-time terrains. Temporal cartography, resonance projections,
       and constellation-scale map fusion ensure that sweep choreography,
       harmonic-lock, and multi-agent synchronization remain stable across
       long mission timelines.

